-- SH-05 — the canonicalizer, seed.
--
-- Pass 48 §4.1 makes layout a compiler stage: the formatter computes it, there
-- is no configuration, and a diff is post-canonical. This is the first stage of
-- that, in Duo.
--
-- WHAT MAKES IT VERIFIABLE. A canonicalizer is correct exactly when it changes
-- layout and nothing else, and SH-04 now gives that a machine check:
--
--     proj_program(canonicalize(src)) == proj_program(src)     -- meaning held
--     canonicalize(canonicalize(src)) == canonicalize(src)     -- a fixed point
--
-- The first property is why this file waited for the parser to reach 245/245 on
-- lib/std: a projection oracle that rejects a third of the corpus cannot tell
-- you whether a rewrite preserved meaning. Both are gated over the whole corpus
-- by examples/pass16_formatter_proof.duo.
--
-- SCOPE, stated honestly, and smaller than I first wrote it. This seed applies
-- exactly one rule:
--
--   * the file ends in exactly one newline
--
-- That is not cosmetic: a file without a trailing newline lost its last
-- statement under the self-hosted parser until today, and two lib/std modules
-- were rejected for exactly that.
--
-- Tab expansion and trailing-space stripping were written, proved over 246
-- files, and then REMOVED. The corpus proof passed, but the fixpoint negative
-- control showed why that was luck rather than safety: a whitespace rule that
-- treats the file as flat text edits DATA — `rstrip` deletes meaningful
-- trailing spaces inside a `[[ ... ]]` literal, and tab expansion rewrites the
-- contents of quoted strings. lib/std simply contains no such string. The
-- string-aware version that followed preserved meaning but was not a fixpoint
-- on lib/std/jit.duo (one byte longer on the second pass), so it is not correct
-- either.
--
-- The rule that ships is the one whose proof I trust. Block-depth re-indent,
-- one-liner joining and multiline splitting are the layout law proper and need
-- structure the parser has but does not yet hand back; they land behind this
-- harness, each with its own control.

global strm = req "std.str"

canonicalize = (src: str): str
    n: i64 = #src
    if n == 0
        return src
    end
    e: i64 = n
    while e > 0 and string.byte(src, e) == 10
        e -= 1
    end
    if e == 0
        return ""
    end
    strm.sub(src, 1, e) .. "\n"
end
